/* book: lead: Quantization rounds each sample to one of L allowed levels, so a sample becomes one of a finite set of values. A uniform quantizer spaces the levels equally, so a quiet signal gets the same absolute error as a loud one and a much worse signal-to-noise ratio. Speech is mostly quiet, so telephony gives small samples fine steps and large samples coarse steps; companding is the practical way to do thi */
